[38;2;124;124;124mthe obstruction better. The key obstruction seems to be large primes in $(n/2, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mn]$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this more carefully. For $n \ge 11$, there's always a prime $[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\ell \in (n/2, n]$ with $\ell - 2$ not prime and no way to get $\ell | (p+q)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me think about this differently. The largest prime $p_{\max} \le [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mn$ is always in $(n/2, n]$ by Bertrand's postulate (for $n \ge 7$). For [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$p_{\max}$ to divide $P(n)$, we need $p_{\max} - 2$ prime (twin prime) or [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$p_{\max} \le 2n/3$ with a suitable representation.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $n$ itself is prime, then $p_{\max} = n$, and $p_{\max} > 2n/3$ for $n > 0$, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mso case (c) is impossible. We need $n - 2$ prime. So if $n$ is prime and $n-2$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis not prime, $n$ fails.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $n$ is not prime, $p_{\max} < n$. We need $p_{\max} - 2$ prime, or $p_{\max} [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\le 2n/3$ with a representation $p + q = 3p_{\max}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut even if $p_{\max}$ is OK, we need ALL primes in $(n/2, n]$ to be OK.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the density of twin primes. The twin prime conjecture says [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minfinitely many, but they become sparse. For large $n$, the primes in $(n/2, n]$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthat are NOT twin primes (i.e., $\ell - 2$ not prime) will be common, and for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meach such prime, we need an alternative representation.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me think about this more carefully. For a prime $\ell \in (n/2, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mn]$ that is NOT a twin prime ($\ell - 2$ not prime), we need either:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Case (b): a prime $q \in (\ell, n]$ with $2\ell - q$ prime[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Case (c): if $\ell \le 2n/3$, primes $p, q$ with $p + q = 3\ell$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the largest prime $p_{\max}$, case (b) is impossible. So we need case (a) or[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(c).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $n$ prime: $p_{\max} = n > 2n/3$, so only case (a). Need $n-2$ prime.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFor $n$ not prime, $p_{\max} < n$: if $p_{\max} > 2n/3$, only case (a). Need [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$p_{\max} - 2$ prime.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, $p_{\max} > 2n/3$ happens when there's no prime in $(2n/3, n]$... wait, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$p_{\max}$ is the largest prime $\le n$, so $p_{\max} > 2n/3$ iff there's a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprime in $(2n/3, n]$, which is almost always true for large $n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo for most $n$, $p_{\max} > 2n/3$, and we need $p_{\max} - 2$ to be prime. This[0m[38;2;124;124;124mmeans $p_{\max}$ and $p_{\max} - 2$ are twin primes.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut even if $p_{\max}$ is a twin prime, we need ALL other primes in $(n/2, n]$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mto also be covered. The second largest prime $\ell_2$ might not be a twin prime,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mand case (b) for $\ell_2$ requires $q = p_{\max}$ (the only prime above $\ell_2$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mup to $n$), giving $p = 2\ell_2 - p_{\max}$. This needs to be prime.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the conditions become increasingly restrictive as we go down the list of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprimes in $(n/2, n]$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about whether it's possible for large $n$ at all. The number of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprimes in $(n/2, n]$ is roughly $n/(2\ln n)$. For each such prime $\ell$ (except[0m[38;2;124;124;124mpossibly the largest), case (b) gives us a chance: $q$ is the next prime above $[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\ell$, and $p = 2\ell - q$. But $p = 2\ell - q$ is roughly $\ell - (q - \ell)$, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhich is close to $\ell$ minus a small gap. This $p$ needs to be prime, which is[0m[38;2;124;124;124ma condition that becomes harder to satisfy for all primes simultaneously.[0m

