[38;2;124;124;124mFor $n \ge 11$, I want to show there's always a "bad" prime. Let me think about [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhat primes $\ell \in (n/2, n]$ look like.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me try a different approach. Let me think about the problem from [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe perspective of: when does $n! | P(n)$?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $n = 7$: primes are 2, 3, 5, 7. Pairs: (2,3), (2,5), (2,7), (3,5), (3,7), [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(5,7). Sums: 5, 7, 9, 8, 10, 12. Product = 5·7·9·8·10·12 = 302400 = 60 · 5040 = [0m[38;2;124;124;124m60 · 7!.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $n = 7$, the primes in $(7/2, 7] = (3.5, 7]$ are 5 and 7.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $\ell = 7$: $7 - 2 = 5$ is prime. So $2 + 5 = 7$, $v_7 \ge 1$. ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $\ell = 5$: $5 - 2 = 3$ is prime. So $2 + 3 = 5$, $v_5 \ge 1$. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBoth primes in $(n/2, n]$ are twin primes! That's why $n = 7$ works.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $n = 5$: primes in $(5/2, 5] = (2.5, 5]$ are 3 and 5.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $\ell = 5$: $5 - 2 = 3$ is prime. ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $\ell = 3$: $3 - 2 = 1$ not prime. $2\ell = 6 = p + q$? Primes $\le 5$: 2, 3, [0m[38;2;124;124;124m5. $p + q = 6$: $3 + 3$ not allowed ($p < q$). So $v_3(P(5)) = ?$. Actually [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$P(5) = 5 \cdot 7 \cdot 8 = 280$. $v_3(280) = 0$. $v_3(5!) = 1$. So fails.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, but $\ell = 3$ is not in $(n/2, n] = (2.5, 5]$... actually $3 > 2.5$, so [0m
[38;2;124;124;124myes it is. And $3 - 2 = 1$ not prime. $2 \cdot 3 = 6$: need $p + q = 6$ with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprimes $p < q \le 5$. $p = 2, q = 4$ (4 not prime). $p = 3, q = 3$ (not $p < [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mq$). So no. $3 \cdot 3 = 9 > 8$. So $v_3 = 0$. Fails.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $n = 7$, the primes in $(3.5, 7]$ are 5 and 7, both twin primes (5-2=3, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m7-2=5). And the smaller primes 2 and 3 have enough valuation from the many [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpairs.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the key is: for $n = 7$, every prime $\ell \in (n/2, n]$ is a twin prime [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(i.e., $\ell - 2$ is also prime). For $n \ge 8$, this fails.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me verify: for $n = 8$, primes in $(4, 8]$ are 5, 7. Both are twin primes. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBut the bottleneck is $v_2$: $v_2(8!) = 7$, $v_2(P(8)) = 6$. So it fails on 2, [0m
