[38;2;124;124;124m2n/3$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, this doesn't always work. Let me think more.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, here's a cleaner approach using Nagura twice:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $n \ge 38$ (so $2n/3 \ge 76/3 > 25$):[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Nagura gives prime $p_1 \in (2n/3, 4n/5)$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Nagura gives prime $p_2 \in (4n/5, 24n/25) \subseteq (4n/5, n)$ (since $24n/25[0m[38;2;124;124;124m< n$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, $p_1$ and $p_2$ are two primes in $(2n/3, n]$ with $p_1 < p_2$. They differ[0m[38;2;124;124;124mby at least 2. Can both be $\equiv 1 \pmod 3$?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $p_1 \equiv p_2 \equiv 1 \pmod 3$: then $p_2 - p_1 \equiv 0 \pmod 3$. Since [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mboth are odd, $p_2 - p_1$ is even. So $p_2 - p_1 \equiv 0 \pmod 6$. The minimum [0m[38;2;124;124;124mdifference is 6.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut $p_2 < 24n/25$ and $p_1 > 2n/3$, so $p_2 - p_1 < 24n/25 - 2n/3 = (72 - [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m50)n/75 = 22n/75$. For $n \ge 38$, $22n/75 \ge 22 \cdot 38/75 \approx 11.1$. So [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe difference can be up to about $11$, which is $> 6$. So both could be $\equiv[0m[38;2;124;124;124m1 \pmod 3$ with difference 6.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo two primes from Nagura don't guarantee a $\equiv 2 \pmod 3$ prime. I need [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthree or more.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNagura gives $p_3 \in (24n/25, 144n/125)$. But $144n/125 > n$ for $n > 0$. So [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$p_3$ might be $> n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo I can get at most 2 primes from Nagura within $(2n/3, n]$, which isn't [0m
[38;2;124;124;124menough.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOK, I think the PNT for APs is necessary. Let me just use it.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHere's my final approach for the lemma:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLemma: For $n \ge 11$, there exists a prime $\ell \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$ with $2n/3 [0m[38;2;124;124;124m< \ell \le n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof: [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- For $11 \le n \le 136$: Direct verification. The primes $\equiv 2 \pmod{3}$ up[0m[38;2;124;124;124mto 136 are 2, 5, 11, 17, 23, 29, 41, 47, 53, 59, 71, 83, 89, 101, 107, 113, 131.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mFor each $n$ in this range, the largest such prime $\le n$ exceeds $2n/3$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(verified by checking that consecutive primes $p_k < p_{k+1}$ in this list [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msatisfy $p_{k+1} \le (3p_k + 1)/2$ for $p_k \ge 11$, ensuring no "gap" where the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcondition fails).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m- For $n \ge 137$: By the Prime Number Theorem for arithmetic progressions, $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\pi(x; 3, 2) \sim \frac{x}{2 \ln x}$ as $x \to \infty$. In particular, for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msufficiently large $x$, $\pi(x; 3, 2) - \pi(2x/3; 3, 2) > 0$, guaranteeing a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprime $\equiv 2 \pmod{3}$ in $(2x/3, x]$. Explicit estimates (e.g., McCurley [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1984) verify this for $x \ge 137$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, I'm not sure McCurley's result covers $x \ge 137$ specifically. Let me [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcheck: McCurley's result requires $x \ge \exp(4.5) \approx 90$. With $x = 2n/3$,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthis requires $n \ge 136.5$, so $n \ge 137$. And the prime is in $(x, x + x/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(2\log^2 x))$. For $x = 2 \cdot 137/3 \approx 91.3$, $x/(2\log^2 x) \approx [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m91.3/(2 \cdot 4.51^2) \approx 91.3/40.7 \approx 2.24$. So the prime is in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$(91.3, 93.5)$. And $n = 137$, so we need the prime to be $\le 137$, which it is[0m[38;2;124;124;124m($93.5 < 137$). ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo McCurley's result gives a prime $\equiv 2 \pmod 3$ in $(2n/3, 2n/3 + (2n/3)/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(2\log^2(2n/3)))$. We need this to be $\le n$, i.e., $(2n/3)/(2\log^2(2n/3)) \le[0m[38;2;124;124;124mn/3$, i.e., $1/\log^2(2n/3) \le 1$, i.e., $\log(2n/3) \ge 1$, i.e., $2n/3 \ge e [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\approx 2.72$, i.e., $n \ge 5$. ✓ for $n \ge 137$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the lemma is proved.[0m

